# ${name}

## Getting started

Change anything you like. Push to `main` and the pipeline builds a container image and pushes it
to your registry:

```
${registry}
```

Every push produces two tags — the commit SHA, and `latest`.

## What is already set up

- **`Dockerfile`** and **`main.py`** — a working web app you can run and replace.
- **`.github/workflows/build.yaml`** — the pipeline. It signs in to the registry and pushes.
- **Repository secrets and variables** — the credentials and registry address the pipeline needs.
  You do not need to touch these, and the credential can only push to this one registry.

## Running it locally

```bash
docker build -t ${name} .
docker run -p 8080:8080 ${name}
```

Then open <http://localhost:8080>.

## Watching a build

The **Actions** tab. A green run ends with a summary saying exactly which tag it pushed.

If a build fails on the push step, it is almost always the registry credential rather than your
code — ask whoever set up your project.
